      "keyInsight": "Whenever a big disruption hits, our first instinct is to protect our old identity. We cling to our former job title or old habits like an outgrown winter coat. But nature never clings to old shells. A tree drops its dry autumn leaves so it can survive the frost and bloom again. The secret to making meaning during chaos is rewriting your inner narrative. You are not a statue carved in stone. You are an ongoing work in progress. When the script changes beneath your feet, the fastest way forward is to grab the pen and start drafting the next scene.",
      "content": "Think about what happens when an experienced chef drops a carton of eggs onto the kitchen floor. A rigid cook freezes up, panics, and ruins dinner. A creative cook sweeps up the mess, pulls out fresh ingredients, and whips up a fantastic stir-fry instead. Life operates by the exact same rule of rapid adaptation.\n\nMajor life disruptions knock our familiar stories off track. Suddenly, the plan you spent five years building no longer fits reality. This is where meaning-making comes in. Meaning is not something lying on the ground waiting to be found. It is something you actively build, piece by piece, out of whatever materials are in front of you.\n\nImagine repacking a backpack in the middle of a sudden rainstorm. You set aside the soaked maps and pull out your waterproof gear. You reassess what truly matters for the miles ahead. In times of change, holding onto your old identity only slows you down. Letting go allows your hands to be free for what comes next.\n\nWhen you treat your story as editable, change stops feeling like an ambush. You become the author rather than a trapped spectator. Every fresh chapter gives you a chance to highlight lessons you never could have learned while sitting comfortably on the couch.",
