      "headline": "Dancing in the Rain: The Three Rooms of Reinvention",
      "keyInsight": "Most people assume a life transition has a strict starting bell and an obvious finish line. We imagine walking across a narrow footbridge from point A to point B. But real transitions do not work in orderly sequence. They are messy, overlapping workshops with three distinct zones: the Long Goodbye, the Messy Middle, and the New Beginning. Counterintuitively, you rarely visit them in order. You might celebrate a fresh start on Monday, fall back into grieving an old loss on Wednesday, and test wild ideas on Friday. Mastering change is not about rushing to the exit. It is about learning to navigate the storm with steady curiosity.",
      "content": "Imagine remodeling a house while you still live inside it. The living room has no drywall, the kitchen counters are bare, and tools sit everywhere. If you demand immediate perfection, you will lose your mind within two days. But if you understand that sawdust is simply part of construction, you can actually enjoy watching the new room take shape.\n\nFeiler found that deep life transitions typically take several years, not a weekend workshop. They are composed of three distinct phases: saying goodbye to what was, wandering through the messy middle, and launching what comes next. The big surprise is that you do not take these steps in a tidy, predictable line.\n\nOne day you might feel energized, brainstorming brand-new ventures like an excited inventor in a busy lab. The next morning, a wave of nostalgia catches you by surprise, pulling you right back to what you left behind. That back-and-forth swing is completely normal. It means your internal system is adjusting to a brand-new climate.\n\nInstead of checking your watch and wondering why you have not arrived yet, lean into the process. Give yourself permission to experiment with fresh habits, tinker with new projects, and test new routines. Transitions are not empty dead zones between real chapters. They are where your most resilient growth actually happens.",
