      "id": "life-is-in-the-transitions-ch-7",
      "order": 7,
      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Accept It: Identify Your Emotions",
      "keyInsight": "When life flips upside down, most people slam on the emotional gas pedal. They want to rush past the pain and fix everything by lunchtime. Here is the counterintuitive twist: rushing makes the engine overheat. Think of big feelings like warning lights on your car dashboard. If the check-engine light flashes bright red, slapping tape over the plastic screen does not fix the motor. You actually have to pull over to the shoulder. Pop the hood open. Look right at the steam. Bruce Feiler found that people who navigate huge life disruptions do not pretend to be superheroes. Instead, they do something disarmingly simple: they name what hurts. Giving an emotion an exact label acts like a pressure release valve on an old steam pipe. It drains the panic out of the room. When you call out fear, sadness, or shock by name, your brain stops treating those feelings like five-alarm house fires. You cannot chart a fresh route on a road map until you admit where you are parked right now.",
      "content": "Imagine you are driving home during a sudden summer storm. The sky turns pitch black. Hail bounces off the metal roof. Your wipers cannot clear the water fast enough. Your gut instinct screams at you to grip the wheel tighter and stomp the accelerator. Yet any seasoned driver knows that is how cars spin into ditches. The only smart move is to pull onto the gravel shoulder, click on your hazard lights, and wait out the downpour. Big life shakeups feel just like that blinding rainstorm. Before you try to reorganize your entire career or repair your routine, you must first accept that your windshield is completely washed out.\n\nBruce Feiler discovered that the very first phase of a massive life transition is not taking bold action. It is acknowledging the mess inside your chest. When a job disappears or a long relationship ends, we usually feel a weird soup of dread, shame, and confusion. We treat these feelings like rude houseguests. We try to lock the deadbolt and pretend nobody is knocking. But ignored emotions do not politely wander away into the yard. They sneak through the basement windows. They turn into sleepless nights, sudden anger over spilled milk, and constant exhaustion.\n\nThink of your feelings as raw weather systems passing over a garden. You cannot negotiate with a cold front. You cannot shout at a thunderstorm to go away. A wise gardener simply looks at the dark clouds, puts on a rain jacket, and secures the greenhouse windows. When you label what you feel out loud\u2014admitting 'I feel terrified' or 'I feel deeply rejected'\u2014the emotional fog starts clearing. You stop wasting your precious energy fighting reality. You finally plant your feet on solid ground so you can figure out what to pack next.",
